# Two signals crossing: west-east stays at y=1, north-south climbs over it.
# The build may put a repeater in the flat (west-east) arm the signal enters by,
# as in the wire tile; the climbing arm has no room for one. The tile is placed
# turned a quarter when the north-south signal is the one that needs a repeater.
# The stone block above the crossing point is what keeps the two apart: a block
# that conducts above a wire stops that wire from connecting diagonally upward,
# and the dust on top of the stone only powers it weakly, which the wire below
# cannot read. The two steps stand on stone, not glass: a signal only steps
# down onto a block that conducts. Those stones are beside the west-east wire,
# which is safe because the wire reads neither weakly powered blocks nor, with a
# conducting block above it, anything diagonal.
tile cross
size 5 4 5
port W io 0 1 2
port E io 4 1 2
port N io 2 1 0
port S io 2 1 4
function cross

legend
  G minecraft:glass
  # minecraft:stone
  - minecraft:redstone_wire

layer 0
  ..G..
  .....
  GGGGG
  .....
  ..G..
layer 1
  ..-..
  ..#..
  -----
  ..#..
  ..-..
layer 2
  .....
  ..-..
  ..#..
  ..-..
  .....
layer 3
  .....
  .....
  ..-..
  .....
  .....
